the supplied literature supports one contested experimental histidine synthesis, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic routes. Accordingly, P1-P10 are ten distinct candidate reaction sequences, not ten validated syntheses. They combine erythrose or threose supply, alternative imidazole-product branches, and direct aminonitrile hydrolysis or speculative hydantoin processing. The strongest evidence concerns erythrose/formamidine chemistry followed by Strecker conversion (Shen et al., 1987, 1990). Formamidine generation, productive conversion of the glycol and ethanol reservoirs, threose-specific yields, and hydantoin processing remain unresolved. Speculative edges must not be interpreted as established positive-flux reactions. All chemistry is assigned to anoxic surface settings; proposed temperatures and pH ranges are screening windows, not recovered experimental conditions. Nonphotochemical reactions would require protection from destructive surface UV, whereas rxn_011 deliberately uses UV. Glycolaldehyde, formaldehyde, HCN, ammonia, carbon dioxide, and water are external simple feedstocks. Neutral formulas omit acid-base speciation and counterions. Erythrose and threose nodes represent separate racemic diastereomer pools; the target is racemic histidine, with no claimed L-selectivity. Reaction sequences include parallel precursor-preparation steps in dependency order. No complete, experimentally validated simple-feedstock-to-histidine network is claimed. Unconfirmed polymer detections and incomplete hydrothermal or semi-enzymatic proposals are not converted into fictitious reaction edges.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Methanimidamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formamidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4N2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H3,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"rac-(2R,3R)-2,3,4-Trihydroxybutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-Erythrose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"rac-(2S,3R)-2,3,4-Trihydroxybutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-Threose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethane-1,2-diol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethan-1-ol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Imidazole-4-ethanol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Molecular hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C7H8N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-[(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)methyl]imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Histidine hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-Histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-4(6(10)11)1-5-2-8-3-9-5/h2-4H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde aldol coupling: erythrose product channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide acting as a general-base catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K, proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, mildly alkaline concentrated solution; proposed pH 9-11. Both tetrose diastereomer pools and higher condensation products are expected. No selective erythrose yield is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-catalyzed enolization of glycolaldehyde followed by aldol addition to another glycolaldehyde molecule produces a C4 aldose. This node represents the erythrose channel, not the entire formose mixture.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chemically standard aldol chemistry, but its productive geochemical implementation here is not experimentally established by the supplied research. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) identify erythrose and threose as minor, unstable formose products. Starting with a simple C2 feedstock avoids claiming that unseeded formaldehyde readily initiates formose chemistry. Tetrose accumulation and survival remain bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde aldol coupling: threose product channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide acting as a general-base catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K, proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, proposed pH 9-11. This is a competing stereochemical channel of the same aldol chemistry represented by rxn_001; no independent environmental compartment is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alternative relative stereochemistry during glycolaldehyde aldol addition gives the threose diastereomer pool.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The chemical justification is the nonstereoselective formation of tetroses in aldol/formose chemistry, rather than a separate origin-of-life framework. The supplied research discusses threose availability but provides no selective production rate or yield for these proposed conditions (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018). Threose supply is therefore conditional and concentration-limited.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Unresolved HCN-ammonia route to formamidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"No productive temperature established; 300-370 K is only the surface search window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Near ambient, with HCN and ammonia retention unresolved\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Explicitly speculative supply edge. No effective catalyst or demonstrated accumulation regime is provided. Null catalyst does not imply that efficient uncatalyzed synthesis has been demonstrated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal ammonia addition to the nitrile carbon followed by proton transfer would give formamidine. This atom-balanced proposal is not an established reaction sequence under the stated environmental conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and essential to closing the feedstock network. The supplied report describes an HCN/ammonia relationship but does not establish efficient direct formamidine production. Ammonium cyanide formation, HCN oligomerization, and amidine hydrolysis compete. The approximately 0.3 M formamidine used in the histidine work cannot be inferred to arise from this edge; its instability is a central criticism (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine formation of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Experimental value unavailable in the supplied report; surface assignment assumes moderate-temperature aqueous chemistry\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Not supplied; near-ambient pressure assumed for environmental mapping\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately 0.3 M formamidine is reported through later critiques. Exact pH, duration, and erythrose concentration were not recovered. A concentrated, UV-shielded aqueous setting is a hypothesis, not a reproduced geochemical experiment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sugar-amidine condensation, Amadori-type rearrangement, dehydration, and imidazole ring closure produce the correctly substituted aldehyde. This is a lumped product channel rather than an elementary reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The strongest experimentally supported ring-forming edge in this network. Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-acetaldehyde up to 1.6% relative to erythrose, and Shen et al. (1990) subsequently obtained histidine from the reaction mixture. Simultaneous accumulation of unstable tetroses and concentrated, hydrolytically labile formamidine makes the environmental interpretation contested (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate threose-formamidine formation of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K, exploratory range only\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed comparison with the erythrose reaction. No threose-specific productive concentration, pH, reaction time, or yield is established by the supplied report.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An analogous sugar-amidine condensation and rearrangement could erase the tetrose stereocenters during formation of the achiral imidazole-acetaldehyde product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate extension rather than a separately demonstrated histidine synthesis. The supplied discussion argues that loss of tetrose stereochemistry makes alternative C4 aldoses chemically relevant, but it supplies no separate quantified threose result (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018; comparison with Shen et al., 1987). Product distribution and kinetics could differ substantially from erythrose.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine imidazole-glycol product channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Experimental value unavailable in the supplied report\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Not supplied; near-ambient pressure assumed\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Represents a competing product channel of tetrose/amidine chemistry. The precise formulation giving the reported maximum glycol yield is not reconstructed here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation and imidazole ring formation retain a vicinal-diol side chain instead of completing conversion to the acetaldehyde side chain.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol is a reported product of the Shen chemistry, with a maximum yield of 6.8% cited in the supplied research (Shen et al., 1987). That maximum is not assigned to the unspecified conditions in this node. Its occurrence supports a reservoir or sink branch, not its productive conversion to histidine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate threose-formamidine imidazole-glycol product channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K, exploratory range only\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"No target-specific threose experiment or product partitioning is available. Glycol stereochemistry is unresolved in this coarse-grained node.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A proposed analogue of the erythrose condensation forms an imidazole-bearing vicinal diol. Different tetrose configurations could change both yield and diol stereochemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative extension of the experimentally observed erythrose-derived glycol branch (Shen et al., 1987). Discussion of tetrose interchangeability supports testing this reaction but does not establish its outcome (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018). It remains a possible sink unless rxn_010 is independently demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formaldehyde-ammonia formation of imidazole-4-ethanol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Experimental value unavailable in the supplied report\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Not supplied; near-ambient pressure assumed\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous erythrose, formaldehyde, and ammonia chemistry. Exact concentrations, pH, and incubation time must be obtained from the original study.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped sugar-ammonia condensation and carbonyl-rearrangement sequence incorporates a formaldehyde-derived carbon and closes the imidazole ring, retaining a hydroxyethyl side chain.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-ethanol up to 5.4% from the erythrose/formaldehyde/ammonia variant. This supplies an experimentally motivated branch that avoids formamidine at the ring-forming stage. It does not establish histidine production, because conversion of the alcohol to the aldehyde was not demonstrated in the supplied research.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate threose-formaldehyde-ammonia formation of imidazole-4-ethanol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K, exploratory range only\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed substrate-comparison experiment. No threose-specific ethanol yield or successful environmental regime is established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An analogous carbohydrate/ammonia/formaldehyde reaction could form the same achiral imidazole-ethanol product after rearrangement and dehydration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Shen et al. (1987) provide support for the erythrose reaction, not this threose variant. Carbon connectivity and eventual loss of sugar stereochemistry justify testing the alternative, but do not establish its selectivity or efficiency. The unstable-tetrose objection remains applicable (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical pinacol-type conversion of imidazole-glycol to imidazole-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium as a proposed acid catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K, proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic geothermal microenvironment, approximately pH 1-3. Requires a change from alkaline sugar-production conditions. No productive regime is known for this substrate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of the ring-adjacent hydroxyl group as water, followed by a hydride shift from the terminal hydroxymethyl group, could generate the CH2-CHO side chain through a pinacol-type rearrangement.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Explicitly speculative. Shen et al. (1987) suggested possible conversion of the glycol to the aldehyde but did not demonstrate this mechanism. The proposed rearrangement is atom-balanced and requires no molecular oxygen. Competing rearrangements, fragmentation, and imidazole protonation could prevent the desired regioselectivity; this edge is a testable hypothesis, not an enabled prebiotic route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical anoxic photodehydrogenation of imidazole-ethanol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Photoactive sphalerite, ZnS, as an unvalidated semiconductor catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K, proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Photoexcited ZnS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic mineral-water interface with UV photons energetic enough to excite ZnS, nominally wavelengths below approximately 340 nm. Hydrogen escape would be required. No oxygen or ferric oxidant is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed semiconductor holes oxidize the primary alcohol to an aldehyde while photogenerated electrons reduce protons to hydrogen. The net process is light-driven dehydrogenation rather than aerobic oxidation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Explicitly speculative and unsupported for this substrate in the supplied research. Shen et al. (1987) demonstrate the alcohol precursor only. ZnS is chosen for a chemically relevant semiconductor function, not because it has been shown to make histidine. Natural-surface activity, hydrogen-evolution kinetics, photocorrosion, and competing destruction of the imidazole ring are unresolved. No yield or catalytic competence is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker formation of histidine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Experimental value unavailable in the supplied report\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Not supplied; near-ambient pressure assumed\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN and ammonia added to the aldehyde-containing mixture. Exact pH, concentrations, and incubation time are not available in the supplied summary. Cyanohydrin formation and aldehyde loss compete.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible aldehyde-ammonia condensation gives an imine or iminium species, followed by cyanide addition to form the alpha-aminonitrile. Transient imine formation is included within this lumped node.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The combined Strecker conversion to histidine is experimentally supported by Shen et al. (1990), who added HCN and ammonia without isolating the preceding reaction products. The aminonitrile node is a mechanistic intermediate, not a separately quantified isolate established by the supplied report. Competition with cyanohydrin chemistry remains unresolved; Cobb et al. (2015) also identify imidazole-4-acetaldehyde as the corresponding Strecker precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Histidine aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium for the proposed acid-hydrolysis implementation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K, proposed environmental screening range; published workup conditions unavailable\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed prolonged acidic incubation, approximately pH 1-3, after the Strecker stage. These conditions are not attributed to Shen et al. without the original experimental procedure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydration through an amide intermediate, followed by further hydrolysis, produces histidine and releases the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia or ammonium. The amide is not separately resolved because no isolated intermediate is established here.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mechanistic resolution of the reported Strecker endpoint, rather than an independently measured reaction yield. Shen et al. (1990) identified histidine by chromatographic and mass-spectrometric methods at 3.5% relative to erythrose. That overall yield is neither the yield of this edge nor a value to multiply by the 1987 aldehyde yield. Productive hydrolysis under mild geological conditions and independent replication remain open questions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate Bucherer-Bergs-type capture as histidine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360 K, proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Near ambient with dissolved inorganic carbon retained\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed ammonia-rich carbonate/bicarbonate solution, approximately pH 8-10. The neutral CO2 node represents the inorganic-carbon contribution, not exclusive aqueous CO2 speciation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed N-carbamylation of the alpha-aminonitrile by inorganic carbon, followed by cyclization and rearrangement, produces the corresponding hydantoin. This is a lumped Bucherer-Bergs-type transformation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative target-specific extrapolation from general hydantoin-forming chemistry; no supporting histidine-hydantoin experiment is supplied. The branch is chemically motivated because it could trap an aminonitrile-derived carbon skeleton in a cyclic reservoir. Shen et al. (1990) support the related Strecker system, not this additional reaction. Formation of a stable hydantoin could equally create an unproductive sink.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate hydrolysis and decarbamylation of histidine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium as a proposed hydrolysis catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed test window; no productive surface-temperature regime established\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged acidic geothermal incubation, approximately pH 1-2, is a hypothesis. Useful conversion may require conditions harsher than plausible surface pools provide, in which case this edge would have negligible productive flux.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring hydrolysis gives an N-carbamyl amino-acid intermediate; subsequent hydrolysis and decarbamylation release histidine, ammonia, and carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Explicitly speculative for this substrate and setting. General hydantoin hydrolysis supplies a chemical analogy, but the supplied histidine literature does not validate this conversion. Cyclic-urea stability, slow hydrolysis, and degradation of released histidine may defeat the pathway. This branch is not additional evidence for abiotic histidine availability and does not remove the bottlenecks emphasized by V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine route with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Closest to the sole reported experimental backbone. Glycolaldehyde supplies erythrose, proposed HCN/ammonia chemistry supplies formamidine, and the resulting imidazole-acetaldehyde undergoes Strecker conversion. The downstream erythrose-fed laboratory result is supported; the complete feedstock sequence is not, principally because rxn_003 is unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-formamidine route with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A substrate-variant hypothesis using threose instead of erythrose. It shares the same aldehyde and Strecker endpoint but lacks a separately quantified threose-to-histidine demonstration. Formamidine supply and tetrose survival remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-derived glycol reservoir followed by Strecker conversion\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the experimentally motivated imidazole-glycol branch, followed by an un demonstrated, regioselective pinacol-type rearrangement to the required aldehyde. Productive flux depends on validating rxn_010; glycol detection alone does not validate this route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-derived glycol reservoir followed by Strecker conversion\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines a speculative threose-to-imidazole-glycol extension with the speculative glycol rearrangement. This is a distinct candidate sequence, not an independently supported histidine synthesis, and carries more uncertainty than P3.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formaldehyde-ammonia alcohol route with anoxic photodehydrogenation\",\n      \"description\": \"Avoids the formamidine supply edge by using the reported erythrose/formaldehyde/ammonia alcohol-forming chemistry. Histidine formation nevertheless requires unvalidated ZnS-mediated alcohol photodehydrogenation before the supported Strecker-type endpoint. No mineral-catalyzed histidine synthesis is claimed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-formaldehyde-ammonia alcohol route with anoxic photodehydrogenation\",\n      \"description\": \"A higher-uncertainty analogue of P5. Both threose-dependent alcohol production and productive photodehydrogenation require demonstration. The route is atom-connected and oxygen-free but cannot presently be assigned a defensible yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose-formamidine route with hydantoin capture and release\",\n      \"description\": \"Branches from the Strecker aminonitrile into a proposed hydantoin reservoir before hydrolytic release of histidine. The ring-forming backbone has experimental precedent, but neither target-specific hydantoin formation nor geochemically productive hydantoin hydrolysis is established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose-formamidine route with hydantoin capture and release\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the threose substrate hypothesis with speculative hydantoin processing. It is sequence-distinct from direct nitrile hydrolysis, but does not constitute a second experimentally established histidine-production mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose glycol-reservoir route with hydantoin capture and release\",\n      \"description\": \"A low-confidence combination of two proposed reservoir-processing mechanisms: glycol rearrangement and hydantoin formation/hydrolysis. It is included as a testable branch combination, not as evidence that either reservoir actually supplies prebiotic histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Threose glycol-reservoir route with hydantoin capture and release\",\n      \"description\": \"The most conjectural listed sequence: threose-to-glycol formation, glycol rearrangement, and hydantoin processing all require validation, in addition to resolving formamidine supply. Its graph connectivity must not be confused with demonstrated chemical feasibility.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_016\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine", "overall_strategy": "Evidence-limited network: the supplied literature supports one contested experimental histidine synthesis, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic routes. Accordingly, P1-P10 are ten distinct candidate reaction sequences, not ten validated syntheses. They combine erythrose or threose supply, alternative imidazole-product branches, and direct aminonitrile hydrolysis or speculative hydantoin processing. The strongest evidence concerns erythrose/formamidine chemistry followed by Strecker conversion (Shen et al., 1987, 1990). Formamidine generation, productive conversion of the glycol and ethanol reservoirs, threose-specific yields, and hydantoin processing remain unresolved. Speculative edges must not be interpreted as established positive-flux reactions. All chemistry is assigned to anoxic surface settings; proposed temperatures and pH ranges are screening windows, not recovered experimental conditions. Nonphotochemical reactions would require protection from destructive surface UV, whereas rxn_011 deliberately uses UV. Glycolaldehyde, formaldehyde, HCN, ammonia, carbon dioxide, and water are external simple feedstocks. Neutral formulas omit acid-base speciation and counterions. Erythrose and threose nodes represent separate racemic diastereomer pools; the target is racemic histidine, with no claimed L-selectivity. Reaction sequences include parallel precursor-preparation steps in dependency order. No complete, experimentally validated simple-feedstock-to-histidine network is claimed. Unconfirmed polymer detections and incomplete hydrothermal or semi-enzymatic proposals are not converted into fictitious reaction edges.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CH4N2", "iupac_name": "Methanimidamide", "common_name": "Formamidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4N2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H3,2,3)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "rac-(2R,3R)-2,3,4-Trihydroxybutanal", "common_name": "DL-Erythrose", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "rac-(2S,3R)-2,3,4-Trihydroxybutanal", "common_name": "DL-Threose", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethanal", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "1-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethane-1,2-diol", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-glycol", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)ethan-1-ol", "common_name": "Imidazole-4-ethanol", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Molecular hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C6H8N4", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "Histidine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C7H8N4O2", "iupac_name": "5-[(1H-Imidazol-4-yl)methyl]imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Histidine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Histidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-4(6(10)11)1-5-2-8-3-9-5/h2-4H,1,7H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Glycolaldehyde aldol coupling: erythrose product channel", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide acting as a general-base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K, proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, mildly alkaline concentrated solution; proposed pH 9-11. Both tetrose diastereomer pools and higher condensation products are expected. No selective erythrose yield is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Base-catalyzed enolization of glycolaldehyde followed by aldol addition to another glycolaldehyde molecule produces a C4 aldose. This node represents the erythrose channel, not the entire formose mixture.", "reasoning": "Chemically standard aldol chemistry, but its productive geochemical implementation here is not experimentally established by the supplied research. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) identify erythrose and threose as minor, unstable formose products. Starting with a simple C2 feedstock avoids claiming that unseeded formaldehyde readily initiates formose chemistry. Tetrose accumulation and survival remain bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Glycolaldehyde aldol coupling: threose product channel", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide acting as a general-base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K, proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, proposed pH 9-11. This is a competing stereochemical channel of the same aldol chemistry represented by rxn_001; no independent environmental compartment is required."}, "mechanism": "Alternative relative stereochemistry during glycolaldehyde aldol addition gives the threose diastereomer pool.", "reasoning": "The chemical justification is the nonstereoselective formation of tetroses in aldol/formose chemistry, rather than a separate origin-of-life framework. The supplied research discusses threose availability but provides no selective production rate or yield for these proposed conditions (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018). Threose supply is therefore conditional and concentration-limited."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Unresolved HCN-ammonia route to formamidine", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "No productive temperature established; 300-370 K is only the surface search window", "pressure": "Near ambient, with HCN and ammonia retention unresolved", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Explicitly speculative supply edge. No effective catalyst or demonstrated accumulation regime is provided. Null catalyst does not imply that efficient uncatalyzed synthesis has been demonstrated."}, "mechanism": "Formal ammonia addition to the nitrile carbon followed by proton transfer would give formamidine. This atom-balanced proposal is not an established reaction sequence under the stated environmental conditions.", "reasoning": "Speculative and essential to closing the feedstock network. The supplied report describes an HCN/ammonia relationship but does not establish efficient direct formamidine production. Ammonium cyanide formation, HCN oligomerization, and amidine hydrolysis compete. The approximately 0.3 M formamidine used in the histidine work cannot be inferred to arise from this edge; its instability is a central criticism (V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Erythrose-formamidine formation of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Experimental value unavailable in the supplied report; surface assignment assumes moderate-temperature aqueous chemistry", "pressure": "Not supplied; near-ambient pressure assumed for environmental mapping", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately 0.3 M formamidine is reported through later critiques. Exact pH, duration, and erythrose concentration were not recovered. A concentrated, UV-shielded aqueous setting is a hypothesis, not a reproduced geochemical experiment."},