ker chemistry is consistent with the racemic target. The principal unresolved issue is operating-condition validation: no numerical temperature, pH or reaction-time windows actually appear in the submitted reaction records. In particular, rxn_004 and especially rxn_005 cannot be assumed to proceed sufficiently rapidly in mildly conditioned surface water. Their required acid/base conditions, heating or extended residence times must be established rather than inferred from the successful net laboratory synthesis. For rxn_001, productive condensation must be evaluated against formamidine hydrolysis under the proposed conditions. Scores for rxn_006 through rxn_009 assess the connected competing branches, not additional steps in P1.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Recover the primary experimental protocols and specify reaction-specific temperature, pH, concentrations, residence times and reagent-addition sequence. Distinguish any analytical or preparative hydrolysis workup from chemistry demonstrated in the proposed environment.\",\n        \"For rxn_004 and rxn_005, establish hydrolysis rates and histidine recovery under explicitly justified surface conditions. If a separate hydrolysis stage is required, document its conditions and physical implementation.\",\n        \"Quantify productive rxn_001 flux relative to rxn_008 and other sugar losses, and determine Strecker selectivity relative to rxn_009. Treat imine formation and cyanohydrin formation as reversible equilibria in any kinetic model.\",\n        \"Encode the inferred coefficients explicitly: rxn_001 produces 3 H2O; rxn_006 produces 2 H2O; rxn_007 consumes 2 NH3 and produces 4 H2O; rxn_008 consumes 2 H2O and produces 2 NH3. These are documentation corrections, not conservation violations.\",\n        \"Repair the unparseable mol_011 InChI and provide a validated histi