rolysis of HCN polymers, approximately 6 M HCl at 373-383 K for 16-24 h; those conditions must not be misattributed to Shen or treated as proof of natural-pool hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Histidinamide hydrolysis to racemic histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium, general-acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 0.5-2.0 and sampling over 1-30 days under anoxic, UV-shielded conditions. This can continue the acidic stage of rxn_003; a further environmental transfer is not required. Measure histidine survival alongside amide disappearance. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; the graph uses neutral reference formulas.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalysed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and C\u2013N cleavage produce the carboxylic acid and ammonia/ammonium. Balanced neutral-species net: C6H10N4O + H2O -> C6H9N3O2 + NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The final histidine endpoint is experimentally supported by Shen et al. (1990), including chromatographic and mass-spectrometric identification. Assignment of a discrete histidinamide intermediate and the proposed operating window are mechanistic extrapolations, not separately reported results. The combined neutral-species hydrolysis is aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. Unknown hydrolysis rates, target degradation and the possibility of an environmentally unrealistic analytical workup prevent assigning a natural production flux. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) therefore remain relevant to the plausibility assessment despite the demonstrated laboratory endpoint.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing erythrose\u2013formamidine formation of imidazole glycol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K, proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic and UV-shielded. Screen alongside rxn_001 at proposed pH 8-10 with erythrose 0.01-0.10 M and approximately 0.30 M total formamidine/formamidinium. Quantify glycol and aldehyde in the same time series; do not assume their separately reported maximum yields occur together.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An alternative sugar\u2013amidine cyclization/dehydration channel retains the oxygenated two-carbon substituent. Balanced neutral-species net: C4H8O4 + CH4N2 -> C5H8N2O2 + 2 H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-glycol, with a maximum reported yield of approximately 6.8% in the investigated chemistry. The supplied report describes glycol as a companion product but does not provide a common-condition branching ratio against aldehyde formation. This node is therefore a reported side product and possible reservoir, not an established productive histidine precursor. Its selective conversion to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde is unproven and is deliberately excluded.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose\u2013formaldehyde\u2013ammonia formation of imidazole ethanol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K, proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic and UV-shielded. Proposed pH 8-10, erythrose and formaldehyde each 0.01-0.10 M, total ammonia/ammonium 0.10-1.0 M and sampling over 1-48 h. These concentrations and times are test conditions, not recovered historical values.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Multistep carbohydrate\u2013aldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation forms the imidazole ring and hydroxyethyl side chain. Balanced neutral-species net: C4H8O4 + CH2O + 2 NH3 -> C5H8N2O + 4 H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-ethanol from erythrose, formaldehyde and ammonia at a maximum yield of approximately 5.4% relative to erythrose. This supports formation of the alcohol, not completion of a histidine route. Under the required anoxic conditions, alcohol-to-aldehyde oxidation cannot be assumed; no unsupported photodehydrogenation, oxidant or mineral catalyst is introduced. The alcohol is retained as a competing product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing net hydrolysis of formamidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K for comparison with rxn_001; proposed kinetic measurement window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Measure at the same pH, ionic strength and temperature as the productive condensation. Proposed pH coverage 8-10, with sampling starting immediately after dissolution. Water is the reactant; no external catalyst is assigned. A measured lifetime is required before concentration by evaporation can be evaluated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential aqueous amidine hydrolysis, potentially through an amide, is represented as a net loss reaction. Balanced neutral-species net: CH4N2 + 2 H2O -> CH2O2 + 2 NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) identify rapid hydrolysis of formamidine to formic acid and ammonia as a central objection to the approximately 0.30 M requirement. They do not provide the condition-specific rate constant needed here. Quantitatively, if disappearance is approximately first order with measured half-life t_half in hours, maintaining 0.30 M against hydrolysis alone requires a replenishment rate of at least 0.30 ln(2)/t_half, approximately 0.208/t_half mol L^-1 h^-1, before productive consumption and other losses. No half-life or replenishment flux is invented. Formation of approximately 0.30 M total formamidine also corresponds to approximately 13.2 g L^-1 on a neutral-formamidine mass basis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing cyanohydrin formation from imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K, proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Measure concurrently with rxn_002 at proposed pH 8.0-9.0 and total HCN/CN 0.01-0.10 M. Cyanohydrin formation is reversible; the graph displays the forward competing channel without assuming irreversible sequestration. Maintain anoxia and UV shielding.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer forms the alpha-hydroxynitrile. Balanced neutral-species net: C5H6N2O + CHN -> C6H7N3O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative for this substrate under the stated window: standard aldehyde\u2013cyanide chemistry makes this a chemically justified competitor, and the supplied discussion of the Shen et al. (1990) route identifies cyanohydrin competition as unresolved. No substrate-specific yield or equilibrium constant is supplied. Its purpose is to expose a selectivity limitation rather than create an additional target pathway. Quantification requires separate measurements of aminonitrile formation, cyanohydrin formation and dissociation, and aldehyde degradation; an assumed quantitative Strecker branch would be unjustified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Potential cyanohydrin hydrolysis to imidazolelactic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium, general-acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 0.5-2.0 with sampling over 1-30 days during the same acidic treatment considered for rxn_003 and rxn_004. Anoxic and UV-shielded. Compare hydrolysis with cyanohydrin dissociation and decomposition; completion is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis retain the alpha-hydroxyl group and release ammonia/ammonium. Balanced neutral-species net: C6H7N3O + 2 H2O -> C6H8N2O3 + NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative competing sink: generic cyanohydrin hydrolysis predicts an alpha-hydroxy acid, but the supplied research does not demonstrate this particular conversion or quantify imidazolelactic acid in the Shen et al. (1990) synthesis. Cyanohydrin dissociation may instead return the aldehyde before hydrolysis. The edge is included to make a chemically credible loss channel explicit, not to claim another histidine synthesis. Its rate and product identity require experimental verification.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Reported erythrose\u2013formamidine\u2013Strecker histidine route\",\n      \"description\": \"The only histidine-producing reaction family supported experimentally by the supplied literature. Supplied D-erythrose and formamidine generate imidazole-4-acetaldehyde; HCN and ammonia permit Strecker chemistry; nitrile hydrolysis yields racemic histidine. Shen et al. (1990) support the overall laboratory endpoint at approximately 3.5% relative to erythrose, not an overall yield from formaldehyde or HCN alone. The aminonitrile and amide steps are mechanistic resolution of that route, not independently quantified demonstrations in the supplied report. The numerical surface operating windows, precursor accumulation, hydrolysis times and competitive fluxes remain unvalidated. No glycol, alcohol, hydrothermal or semi-enzymatic branch is represented as an established additional route.\"\n      ,\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"r