pproximately 1.6% relative to erythrose. This is the central productive bottleneck, not evidence for efficient synthesis from formaldehyde. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2016, 2018) identify unstable, scarce tetroses and rapidly hydrolysing formamidine at approximately 0.30 M as serious geochemical objections. The proposed numerical window requires testing and is not attributed to Shen. Productive operation requires the measured aldehyde-forming timescale to compete with both erythrose degradation and rxn_007; no relevant rate constants are supplied."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Strecker formation of histidine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K, proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar; gas-retaining laboratory comparison required", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic and UV-shielded. Proposed pH 8.0-9.0, total HCN/CN 0.01-0.10 M, total ammonia/ammonium 0.10-1.0 M and sampling over 1-48 h. Add HCN and ammonia after the sugar\u2013amidine stage, rather than assuming all reactions are compatible simultaneously. Open-pool HCN and ammonia retention is unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Reversible aldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation generates an imine/iminium ensemble, followed by cyanide addition. Balanced neutral-species net: C5H6N2O + CHN + NH3 -> C6H8N4 + H2O. Without an enantioselective agent, the new alpha-carbon is expected to be racemic.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. (1990) obtained histidine after adding HCN and ammonia to the unisolated sugar\u2013amidine reaction mixture, reporting approximately 3.5% histidine relative to erythrose. That supports the overall Strecker route, but the supplied report does not establish isolation, yield or kinetics of this aminonitrile. Cobb et al. (2015) also pair histidine with this aldehyde in Strecker modelling. The 1987 aldehyde maximum and 1990 histidine yield come from different experiments and must not be multiplied or interpreted as a common kinetic dataset. Competition with rxn_008, aldehyde degradation and volatile-feedstock loss must be measured; the proposed concentrations do not establish selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Acid-catalysed hydration of histidine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium, general-acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded acidic aqueous stage. Proposed pH 0.5-2.0 with time-course sampling over 1-14 days. This stage requires acidification or transfer to acidic water after Strecker formation. Conversion within this window is not established; mild-pH spontaneous completion is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation and water addition lead through an imidic-acid intermediate to the carboxamide. Balanced neutral-species net: C6H8N4 + H2O -> C6H10N4O.", "reasoning": "Mechanistically inferred, not separately demonstrated in the supplied research: nitrile-to-amide hydration is the expected first part of the hydrolysis required by the Shen et al. (1990) Strecker interpretation. The supplied summary does not report histidinamide isolation or substrate-specific hydrolysis conditions. Consequently, the proposed acidic surface window is a hypothesis rather than a validated implementation. Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) describe much harsher analytical hydrolysis of HCN polymers, approximately 6 M HCl at 373-383 K for 16-24 h; those conditions must not be misattributed to Shen or treated as proof of natural-pool hydrolysis."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Histidinamide hydrolysis to racemic histidine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium, general-acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 0.5-2.0 and sampling over 1-30 days under anoxic, UV-shielded conditions. This can continue the acidic stage of rxn_003; a further environmental transfer is not required. Measure histidine survival alongside amide disappearance. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; the graph uses neutral reference formulas."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalysed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and C\u2013N cleavage produce the carboxylic acid and ammonia/ammonium. Balanced neutral-species net: C6H10N4O + H2O -> C6H9N3O2 + NH3.", "reasoning": "The final histidine endpoint is experimentally supported by Shen et al. (1990), including chromatographic and mass-spectrometric identification. Assignment of a discrete histidinamide intermediate and the proposed operating window are mechanistic extrapolations, not separately reported results. The combined neutral-species hydrolysis is aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. Unknown hydrolysis rates, target degradation and the possibility of an environmentally unrealistic analytical workup prevent assigning a natural production flux. V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) therefore remain relevant to the plausibility assessment despite the demonstrated laboratory endpoint."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Competing erythrose\u2013formamidine formation of imidazole glycol", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K, proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic and UV-shielded. Screen alongside rxn_001 at proposed pH 8-10 with erythrose 0.01-0.10 M and approximately 0.30 M total formamidine/formamidinium. Quantify glycol and aldehyde in the same time series; do not assume their separately reported maximum yields occur together."}, "mechanism": "An alternative sugar\u2013amidine cyclization/dehydration channel retains the oxygenated two-carbon substituent. Balanced neutral-species net: C4H8O4 + CH4N2 -> C5H8N2O2 + 2 H2O.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-glycol, with a maximum reported yield of approximately 6.8% in the investigated chemistry. The supplied report describes glycol as a companion product but does not provide a common-condition branching ratio against aldehyde formation. This node is therefore a reported side product and possible reservoir, not an established productive histidine precursor. Its selective conversion to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde is unproven and is deliberately excluded."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Erythrose\u2013formaldehyde\u2013ammonia formation of imidazole ethanol", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K, proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic and UV-shielded. Proposed pH 8-10, erythrose and formaldehyde each 0.01-0.10 M, total ammonia/ammonium 0.10-1.0 M and sampling over 1-48 h. These concentrations and times are test conditions, not recovered historical values."}, "mechanism": "Multistep carbohydrate\u2013aldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation forms the imidazole ring and hydroxyethyl side chain. Balanced neutral-species net: C4H8O4 + CH2O + 2 NH3 -> C5H8N2O + 4 H2O.", "reasoning": "Shen et al. (1987) reported imidazole-4-ethanol from erythrose, formaldehyde and ammonia at a maximum yield of approximately 5.4% relative to erythrose. This supports formation of the alcohol, not completion of a histidine route. Under the required anoxic conditions, alcohol-to-aldehyde oxidation cannot be assumed; no unsupported photodehydrogenation, oxidant or mineral catalyst is introduced. The alcohol is retained as a competing product."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Competing net hydrolysis of formamidine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K for comparison with rxn_001; proposed kinetic measurement window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Measure at the same pH, ionic strength and temperature as the productive condensation. Proposed pH coverage 8-10, with sampling starting immediately after dissolution. Water is the reactant; no external catalyst is assigned. A measured lifetime is required before concentration by evaporation can be evaluated."}, "mechanism": "Sequential aqueous amidine hydrolysis, potentially through an amide, is represented as a net loss reaction. Balanced neutral-species net: CH4N2 + 2 H2O -> CH2O2 + 2 NH3.", "reasoning": "V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al. (2018) identify rapid hydrolysis of formamidine to formic acid and ammonia as a central objection to the approximately 0.30 M requirement. They do not provide the condition-specific rate constant needed here. Quantitatively, if disappearance is approximately first order with measured half-life t_half in hours, maintaining 0.30 M against hydrolysis alone requires a replenishment rate of at least 0.30 ln(2)/t_half, approximately 0.208/t_half mol L^-1 h^-1, before productive consumption and other losses. No half-life or replenishment flux is invented. Formation of approximately 0.30 M total formamidine also corresponds to approximately 13.2 g L^-1 on a neutral-formamidine mass basis."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Competing cyanohydrin formation from imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K, proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Measure concurrently with rxn_002 at proposed 